SUMMARY:Applied Mathematics Seminar: Paul Laiu — Structure-preserving acc
 eleration methods for kinetic simulations
LOCATION:GP 346\, The University of Alabama\, Tuscaloosa\, Alabama
DESCRIPTION:Paul Laiu\, Staff Mathematician\, Oak Ridge National Laboratory
 \nStructure-preserving acceleration methods for kinetic simulations\nKinet
 ic equations model physical phenomena across neutron transport\, plasmas\,
  and radiative transfer. However\, large-scale kinetic simulations are bot
 tlenecked by severe memory and computational costs due to their high dimen
 sionality. In this talk\, we will discuss two different acceleration strat
 egies that substantially reduce these costs while preserving important str
 uctural properties. First\, we introduce a new entropy-based moment closur
 e for moment systems derived from kinetic equations. This new closure bypa
 sses expensive optimization in traditional entropy-based closures via a co
 nvex neural network approximation of the entropy function. This approach e
 nables efficient\, high-order moment simulations while guaranteeing provab
 le physical properties such as entropy dissipation and hyperbolicity. Seco
 nd\, we combine a high-order/low-order (HOLO) method with a micro-macro (M
 M) decomposition to accelerate iterative solvers in implicit time-stepping
  schemes for the Bhatnagar-Gross-Krook (BGK) model. The HOLO method improv
 es convergence by incorporating source-term estimates from a coupled low-o
 rder model\, whereas the MM decomposition reduces the memory cost at the f
 luid limit. Numerical experiments confirm that both strategies deliver sig
 nificant savings in memory and computation over traditional methods.\nhttp
 s://jiazhaomath.github.io/seminar/2026-2027.html#2026-11-06
URL:https://jiazhaomath.github.io/seminar/2026-2027.html#2026-11-06
